 One Comeback", "summary": "Opus 5.5 won as Black by 4 points after the 12k, in turn, handed back nearly everything. Opus 5.5 was ahead only narrowly in the opening, then fell to -31.1 by move 31 after a run of big losses. The 12k answered with errors of its own at moves 32, 36 and 50, each worth 13 points or more, and the lead swung back to roughly even. The endgame settled at +4.0 for Black, and both sides passed.", "moments": [{"turn": 27, "text": "Opus 5.5 played D5 and the lead dropped from -1.9 to -15.4, a loss of 13.5 points. The engine preferred D7. This is where a close middle game became a real deficit."}, {"turn": 31, "text": "B7 lost 15.9 points and took Black's lead from -15.3 to -31.1, the lowest point of the game. The engine wanted F4 here instead, on the other side of the board."}, {"turn": 32, "text": "The 12k's A5 gave the game straight back: it lost 18.2 points, moving the lead from -31.1 to -13.0. The engine's choice was F4, the point Opus 5.5 had also left alone a move earlier."}, {"turn": 36, "text": "White's B2 lost 17.7 points, moving the lead from -27.6 to -9.9. The engine preferred D8. Opus 5.5 had been at -27.6 after two big losses of its own, and this reply pulled it back into range."}, {"turn": 50, "text": "A7 by the 12k lost 13.2 points and moved the lead from -15.8 to -2.6. With the engine's choice at B9, which Opus 5.5 took on its next move, the game was effectively level. The last small gains came at moves 52 and 56, and Opus 5.5 closed out the B+4 win by playing the engine's own first choice in the final exchanges."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Every command ran libs.py (groups and liberties of the pasted board) or count.py (area count of the current position), plus appending notes to games.md and CLAUDE.md. Both scripts only analyze the given position with flood fill and never choose, score or look ahead at moves, so they are helpers.", "stop": false}}